NoteGo deeper: ordinal factor analysis is the graded response model





Assumptions: an item with ordered categories \(0, \dots, K\); a latent response \(y^* = \lambda^\top \eta + \varepsilon\) with \(\eta\) multivariate normal (variances 1), \(\varepsilon \sim N(0, 1 - h^2)\) independent of \(\eta\) and of the other items’ errors, where \(h^2 = \text{var}(\lambda^\top\eta)\) is the communality, so \(\text{var}(y^*) = 1\). The response is \(x = k\) when \(\tau_k &lt; y^* \le \tau_{k+1}\), with \(\tau_0 = -\infty\) and \(\tau_{K+1} = \infty\).
Boundary curves. For \(k = 1, \dots, K\), \[\Pr(x \ge k \mid \eta) = \Pr(\varepsilon &gt; \tau_k - \lambda^\top\eta) = \Phi\!\left(\frac{\lambda^\top\eta - \tau_k}{\sqrt{1 - h^2}}\right).\] With one factor this is \(\Phi\big(a(\eta - b_k)\big)\) with \(a = \lambda/\sqrt{1-\lambda^2}\) and \(b_k = \tau_k/\lambda\): one slope per item and one boundary per category, which is Samejima’s (1969) normal-ogive graded response model. Category probabilities are differences of adjacent boundary curves. With several factors, the slope on factor \(m\) is \(a_m = \lambda_m/\sqrt{1 - h^2}\).
Local independence. The errors are independent across items, so given \(\eta\) the responses are independent. Item response theory’s local independence is the factor model’s uncorrelated unique parts.
Logistic metric. \(\sup_z |\Phi(z) - \text{logit}^{-1}(1.702z)| &lt; 0.01\) (the widget’s gap), so a logistic slope is about \(1.702a\); in mirt’s form, \(d_k = -1.702\,a\,b_k\).
Why the numbers differ in practice. The model is the same; the estimators are not. lavaan’s WLSMV estimates thresholds and polychoric correlations, which use the data one or two items at a time (Muthén, 1984; Flora & Curran, 2004). mirt maximizes the likelihood of whole response patterns. When the model holds they converge on the same values as the sample grows; in a finite sample they differ by sampling error, by the logistic approximation, and wherever the model misfits, since the two estimators weigh the misfit differently.



When can Likert items be treated as numbers? In simulations by Rhemtulla, Brosseau-Liard and Savalei (2012), robust maximum likelihood did about as well as the categorical estimator with five or more roughly symmetric categories, and the categorical estimator did better with fewer. Grit has five categories with no pile-up at either end; the DASS-21 has four, most responses at zero. We treat them accordingly.



Does the model hold in both groups?
Do women and men with the same level of anxiety answer an anxiety item the same way? If not, a difference in their scores is partly a difference in the items. Measurement invariance (Meredith, 1993) asks this of a factor model fitted to two or more groups at once, in steps that add constraints (Vandenberg & Lance, 2000; Millsap, 2011; Putnick & Bornstein, 2016):

configural: the same items load on the same factors in every group;
metric: the loadings are equal, so a unit of the factor means the same thing in each group;
scalar: the intercepts (for ordinal items, the thresholds) are equal too, so the same factor level predicts the same response.

Comparing factor correlations across groups needs metric invariance; comparing means needs scalar. When one item fails, partial invariance frees its parameters and keeps the rest equal. In item response language, an unequal loading is non-uniform differential item functioning (DIF) and an unequal intercept is uniform DIF, which Differential item functioning takes up item by item. With ordinal items, which parameters are identified at each step is subtler than with continuous ones (Wu & Estabrook, 2016).

