NoteGo deeper: the score equation and the standard error





Assumptions: independent binary observations \(y_i\); \(p_i = \text{logit}^{-1}(\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i)\). For simplicity, treat \(\beta_0\) as known and write \(\eta_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i\).
The derivative. Since \(\partial p_i/\partial \eta_i = p_i(1-p_i)\) and \(\partial \eta_i/\partial \beta_1 = x_i\), \[\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \beta_1} = \sum_i \left[\frac{y_i}{p_i} - \frac{1-y_i}{1-p_i}\right] p_i(1-p_i)\,x_i = \sum_i \big(y_i(1-p_i) - (1-y_i)p_i\big)x_i = \sum_i (y_i - p_i)\,x_i.\] Setting it to zero gives the score equation. It has no closed-form solution, which is why we need optim() or Newton’s method.
The curvature. Differentiating again, \[\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \beta_1^2} = -\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)\,x_i^2.\] This is always negative, so the log likelihood has a single peak. Its negative is the Fisher information for \(\beta_1\), and the standard error of \(\hat\beta_1\) is approximately \[SE(\hat\beta_1) \approx \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)\,x_i^2}}.\] Two features of this formula recur throughout the course. Observations with \(p_i\) near 0 or 1 contribute almost nothing, because \(p_i(1-p_i)\) is tiny there. And the standard error shrinks as observations accumulate. With both parameters unknown, the same calculation gives a \(2\times 2\) information matrix, and the standard errors come from its inverse; that’s what glm() reports.
When there is no maximum. If some value of \(x\) perfectly separates the successes from the failures, \(\ell\) keeps increasing as \(\beta_1 \to \infty\) and the MLE doesn’t exist (Albert & Anderson, 1984). A respondent who answers every item correctly is the item-response version of this problem; see Estimating abilities.






Maximizing in practice
For logistic regression, glm() does the maximizing for us (it uses a version of Newton’s method). But nothing about the likelihood needs glm(): write \(\ell\) as an R function and hand it to a general optimizer such as optim(), and you get the same answer. The simulation below does exactly that. Later lessons use the same move for models glm() can’t fit, and Estimating item parameters opens up the machinery.


Item responses as a logistic regression






Recall from Item response data and the IRW: IRW tables are long, with one row per respondent–item response and extra columns (such as a respondent covariate) alongside.



One row per response is the shape a logistic regression wants: each row is one binary outcome. So if we have a predictor for each respondent, we can regress responses on it directly. That is what we do with the chess data below: each row is one player’s attempt at one problem, and the predictor is the player’s Elo rating. What makes item responses different from a generic binary outcome is that they come from different items, and items differ. How we account for that matters a great deal.
